  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Located at the far south of Pampelonne beach, the Bonne Terrasse cove was the stronghold of Ramatuelle's fishermen and remains little known to the general public, according to the tourist office. It forms an arc-shaped strip of sand 150 metres long; access is via steps (about 10 minutes from the car park). The beach is not supervised.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Ramatuelle tourist office states in its FAQ that all the town's beaches (Pampelonne bay, Bonne Terrasse cove, L'Escalet beach and the Canadel coves) are open to dogs on a leash. Its beach listing, published on the Grimaud tourist office website and updated by the Ramatuelle office on 6 February 2026, specifies: "Dogs are allowed, on a leash, throughout the year", with free access and open every day. No time slot is announced.</p>
      <p>The office's 2025 "Dog friendly" leaflet adds that swimming is allowed for dogs except in the ZRUBs, the zones reserved solely for swimming; the lifeguard posts can tell you where they are. The commune's posts operate only in season (three on Pampelonne bay, one at L'Escalet), and Bonne Terrasse beach is not supervised: your dog's swimming is at your own responsibility, depending on the weather and the sea.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: access is via steps (about 10 minutes from the car park): check that your dog is comfortable with steps and bring water for him, as no water point is reported.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The coastal path continues to <a href="/en/fiche-ramatuelle">La Douane beach</a>, where the tourist office points out a customs house built under Napoleon I and recently restored, then to <a href="/en/fiche-plage-cap-taillat">Cap Taillat</a> — a nice walk to extend the day on foot with your dog.</p>
    </div>

